I think I see the problem here.  They are confusing  Virtual Address Space
(32 bit VA) with physical memory.  Even though a processor may only have 32
bits of virtual address (4GB), it may have many more or less bits of
physical address.  The two have no relationship to each other.

Secondly, NT is not (nor is any other OS) organized into memory and I/O
space described here.  It is divided into process-private (my term) and
system-shared address spaces.  NT makes this divide using the high-order bit
of the virtual address, thus the 2GB/2GB split.  NT 4.0 Enterprise Edition
running on Inhell processors could be configured to move the split such that
each process had 3GB private and only 1GB shared between all processes and
the kernel.  Alphas had some type of VLM (Very large memory) addressing mode
that allowed memory above 4GB to be addressed.  Others would know more about
this than would I.

NT uses a uniform memory access scheme in which all processors see all
memory in exactly the same way.  There is no memory/cpu "bank switching"
required.  The individual who gave you this information was either thinking
of something completely different.  Intel had such systems many years ago
for doing iRMX/realtime work.  These systems used a bank-switching scheme as
described.  They were rather difficult to program and use.

Greg

