I asked the same question to a salesman from Siemens Nixdorf who wanted to
sell such a 8-way box to me.
He did the best he could do, he forwarded it to his technician. This one did
not know the answer, so he forwarded it to somebody he knew from the design
group of these systems. This guy came out with the result that the way how
NT is organizing the IO space ( 2 GB) and the memory space ( 2 GB) is
modified in a way that these systems can adress effectively 3 GB.
I insisted that I am very pleased to see how to go from 2 to 3 and that I
was very thankful for this information, but I would like to know what the
rest of the 5 GB is intended to do. They promised me to find this out, but
never came back but a small notice on "depends on the application"
Same is true for any other OS, that 4 GB is the maximum you can adress with
a 32 bit processor if the OS only uses little IO space.
These eight way boxes now indeed are two four way boxes interconnected by a
strange crossbar. Thats why you can plug 2x4GB in them. You cannot use them
directly. If an OS would use them, it would have to introduce a banking
scheme. A possible scheme would be( maye the IO space starts at low):

Box1  CPU1-4    Memory adress 00000000 to 3FFFFFFF ( 1 GB direct)
                           Memory adress 80000000 to 9FFFFFFF ( 0.5 GB, 6 x
mapped )
                           memory adress C0000000 to DFFFFFFF ( 0.5 GB IO
space )
Box2  CPU5-8   the same starting at 40000000.

The OS itself would only work in the usual range. 6 GB would be accessible
to (database) applications via special calls running through a set ob banks.
1 GB remains IO space.

You remember that the even switched 10x64KB? They will redo so.
Burkhard

