All,

An Intel based VLM machine could access 8 GB memory using 33 bit memory
address for a specific application (read, something of a hack).  This isn't
true VLM, Oracle has already done this in Oracle 7 VLM version with an
"enhanced" Windows NT Enterprise Edition capable of providing a split of 1
GB system, 3 GB applciation address space (as opposed to 2/2GB).  Its
really not too impressive considering, and its highly applicaiton dependent
(you must provide your own VLM code).

The 8 processor systems becoming so important to the PC Server industry are
targeted at Windows 2000 deployments, not Windows NT 4.0 deployments.
There are lots of bugs to be worked out yet, and early adopters are going
to help (note the last few posts about Corallary chipset being a problem on
some Intel designs).  

The day an 8 processor Intel anything impresses me will be the day I forget
everything I know about 14 and 16 way AlphaServer systems or the soon to be
released WildFire.  NUMA is still a sucky method of implementing SMP
systems, it will take some time before the problems come close to closure.
Here's to another slew of non-events Intel and PC Server builders harp on
as "first of their kind" showstoppers.

Where is Kerry Main?  I desperately need that positive mind posted to this
list again, this group just isn't the same without her.  Calling Kerry!
What is your take on the migration issues, availability of the SRM console
to API and the Slot B clone market and VLM for Linux?

Regards to all,

--Kirk

